  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/59cef258a1ad0e6a0f56c37e0b7020e5341c329d5655c41f49e6e78eafca8b3f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-5,-2)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1,-2).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
